#{[{:activity/expected-seconds 1200.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129967} :activity/id #uuid "df9a25dc-813f-52ef-bbe9-65001555ffea" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050324 :topic/id #uuid "3ffd42a4-cf3d-5ec4-9be0-19d427299cac" :topic/math-academy-id 2014} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129967 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130348 :tutorial/content "Recall that the matrices $[\\mathbf{T}]_{\\color{blue}\\mathcal{B}}$ and $[\\mathbf{T}]_{\\color{red}\\mathcal{C}}$ of a linear transformation $\\mathbf{T} : V \\to V$ relative to two bases $\\color{blue}\\mathcal{B}$ and $\\color{red}\\mathcal{C}$ of a vector space $V$ are different.\n\nIf we are given the matrix $[\\mathbf{T}]_{\\color{blue}\\mathcal{B}},$ how do we find the matrix $[\\mathbf{T}]_{\\color{red}\\mathcal{C}}?$\nTo find $[\\mathbf{T}]_{\\color{red}\\mathcal{C}},$ we use the change-of-coordinates matrix $P_{\\small {\\color{blue}\\mathcal{B}} \\to {\\color{red}\\mathcal{C}}}$ from $\\color{blue}\\mathcal{B}$ to ${\\color{red}\\mathcal{C}}.$\n\nThe diagram below is a commutative diagram that shows how a change of basis works at each step.\n\n![](images/df/dff02d9f46eb7ded23f418765a50dd1971145cd0e585a82beeef1a45812df8a7.png)\n\nHere, moving along an arrow means pre-multiplying by the corresponding matrix. So algebraically, we can write this as\n\n$$\n[\\mathbf{T}]_{\\color{red}\\mathcal{C}} = P_{\\small{\\color{blue}\\mathcal{B}}\\to{\\color{red}\\mathcal{C}}} \\cdot [\\mathbf{T}]_{\\color{blue}\\mathcal{B}} \\cdot P_{\\small{\\color{blue}\\mathcal{B}}\\to{\\color{red}\\mathcal{C}}}^{-1}.\n$$\n\nFor instance, if ![](images/89/89530292f17bb402acb489d90a17e7628a33bab37165b02580479b116e6a2b23.svg) and ![](images/f7/f75100cf6de9ccdb537d685f4e2858278214bbe351c9828e8a94a58628c1151b.svg), we use this formula to obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[T]_{C} & ={P}_{B\\to C}\\cdot [T]_{B}\\cdot {P}_{B\\to C}^{-1} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & -3 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}]{[\\begin{aligned}-1 & -3 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}]}^{-1} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & -3 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 & 3 \\\\ -1 & -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 & 3 \\\\ -1 & -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}5 & 7 \\\\ -2 & -3\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving the formula for $[\\mathbf{T}]_{\\color{blue}\\mathcal{B}},$ we obtain the alternative formulation\n\n$$\n[\\mathbf{T}]_{\\color{blue}\\mathcal{B}} = P_{\\small{\\color{blue}\\mathcal{B}}\\to{\\color{red}\\mathcal{C}}}^{-1} \\cdot [\\mathbf{T}]_{\\color{red}\\mathcal{C}} \\cdot P_{\\small{\\color{blue}\\mathcal{B}}\\to{\\color{red}\\mathcal{C}}}.\n$$\n\n**Note:** Alternatively, if we know the change-of-coordinates matrix $P_{\\small {\\color{red}\\mathcal{C}} \\to {\\color{blue}\\mathcal{B}}}$ from $\\color{red}\\mathcal{C}$ to $\\color{blue}\\mathcal{B},$ we can construct a similar diagram:\n\n![](images/d6/d6cd195ccd42804ea91debabd23563bb0f6903fdd1b5d708b712c75417fff2a9.png)\n\nFrom this, we can derive the equation\n\n$$\n[\\mathbf{T}]_{\\color{red}\\mathcal{C}} = P_{\\small{\\color{red}\\mathcal{C}}\\to{\\color{blue}\\mathcal{B}}}^{-1} \\cdot [\\mathbf{T}]_{\\color{blue}\\mathcal{B}} \\cdot P_{\\small{\\color{red}\\mathcal{C}}\\to{\\color{blue}\\mathcal{B}}}.\n$$\n\nSolving this equation for $[\\mathbf{T}]_{\\color{blue}\\mathcal{B}},$ we obtain the alternative formulation\n\n$$\n[\\mathbf{T}]_{\\color{blue}\\mathcal{B}} =\n\tP_{\\small{\\color{red}\\mathcal{C}}\\to{\\color{blue}\\mathcal{B}}} \\cdot [\\mathbf{T}]_{\\color{red}\\mathcal{C}} \\cdot P_{\\small{\\color{red}\\mathcal{C}}\\to{\\color{blue}\\mathcal{B}}}^{-1}.\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "daf756b8-28df-5435-a304-0c32de77cc02" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3585 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "997bad3a-19a8-5b8c-ba06-b960fea4fabc" :step/math-academy-id 20144 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130108}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129968 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129130 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186128941 :question/id #uuid "76ff3ea8-e07c-5dba-a9af-a4f68b03fadb" :question/math-academy-id "e-5516"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "61bd0b4f-165c-5f31-b4ec-e0eff7e758e1" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049548} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050407}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129630} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129639} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186326682} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360752} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360753} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186375096} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186388702} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186395222} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186395229} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186395236} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186419683} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186480634}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Matrix of a Linear Transformation Relative to a Basis Using a Change-of-Coordinates Matrix"} :step/id #uuid "a9f19301-a016-578d-9241-cc7262d6f30f" :step/math-academy-id 20145 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129970}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129969 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130349 :tutorial/content "Consider the bases $\\color{blue}\\mathcal{B}=\\{ \\mathbf{b}_1, \\, \\ldots, \\, \\mathbf{b}_n \\}$ and $\\color{red}\\mathcal{C}=\\{ \\mathbf{c}_1, \\, \\ldots, \\, \\mathbf{c}_n \\}$ of a vector space $V,$ and the matrix $[\\mathbf{T}]_{\\color{blue}\\mathcal{B}}$ of a linear transformation $\\mathbf{T}$ with respect to the basis $\\color{blue}\\mathcal{B}.$\nThen the matrix of $\\mathbf{T}$ relative to the basis ${\\color{red}\\mathcal{C}}$ is given by\n\n$$\n[\\mathbf{T}]_{\\color{red}\\mathcal{C}} =P_{\\small {\\color{blue}\\mathcal{B}} \\to {\\color{red}\\mathcal{C}}} \\cdot [\\mathbf{T}]_{\\color{blue}\\mathcal{B}} \\cdot P_{\\small {\\color{blue}\\mathcal{B}} \\to {\\color{red}\\mathcal{C}}}^{-1},\n$$\n\nwhere $P_{\\small {\\color{blue}\\mathcal{B}} \\to {\\color{red}\\mathcal{C}}}$ is the change of basis matrix from ${\\color{blue}\\mathcal{B}}$ to ${\\color{red}\\mathcal{C}}.$\n\nWhen written in expanded form, this formula becomes:\n\n![](images/0c/0c782638ef4254884c9521b8c36452d241d4e95acb5f4c54ecd31b9045490143.svg)" :tutorial/id #uuid "f7eacd50-63e4-53d3-9c15-d3a1b6a42ad1" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3586 :tutorial/title "The Expanded Form of the Change of Basis"} :step/id #uuid "22fcb777-1e74-5081-b738-034b4f36efdc" :step/math-academy-id 20146 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130010}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129970 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129131 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186128942 :question/id #uuid "4b287ac7-7569-59bc-a319-0e62996083bc" :question/math-academy-id "e-5517"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "969220db-a686-511b-b33a-0b4f6ab66b1a" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129641} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129642} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272105} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360754} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360755} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360756} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186375598} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186388730} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186392198} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186395243} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186419684} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186480673}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Matrix of a Linear Transformation Relative to the Standard Basis"} :step/id #uuid "ec690891-eb4f-5c61-8dfc-bd14969e4bcd" :step/math-academy-id 20147 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129969}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130010 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129155 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186128973 :question/id #uuid "5e4b6303-8ec5-5ba6-ba1d-3b28ff8924b1" :question/math-academy-id "e-5897"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "86028fae-a119-5194-975d-a6d9fad6faf5" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129697} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129701} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272110} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186326717} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360776} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360777} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360778} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186375116} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186395256} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186395263} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186419685} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186480696}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Matrix of a Linear Transformation Relative to a Given Basis"} :step/id #uuid "b6ae143c-69c0-50ab-a964-29c6f8f34d83" :step/math-academy-id 21621} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130108 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129213 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129032 :question/id #uuid "c269a1d0-a47c-5f5c-96d8-4da2ecd35a48" :question/math-academy-id "e-8563"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "434a82c2-7964-525a-85b9-eb17fa7b5b26" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050008}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129640} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129656} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272063} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272064} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186326833} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360747} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360748} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360749} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360750} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186419686} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186480637}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Connections Between Matrices of a Linear Transformation Relative to Different Bases"} :step/id #uuid "1481fd15-5d4d-5662-9141-ebd88670e6fc" :step/math-academy-id 31802 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129968}}] :activity/title "Connections Between Matrix Representations of a Linear Transformation" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186129357}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046673} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186419688} :activity/id #uuid "a3e8a2c7-af14-540f-a7b1-76dc269d676b" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050324 :topic/id #uuid "3ffd42a4-cf3d-5ec4-9be0-19d427299cac" :topic/math-academy-id 2014} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186419687 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186419684 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0b6934d8-eaf0-5f13-b06a-71ac2a0dc0af" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 3\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186419660} {:answer/id #uuid "5c9c3643-ad71-51e0-bd19-40d272c2cabe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}5 & -3 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186419667} {:answer/id #uuid "8f47a664-a9ad-5deb-90c8-17a7a38adab0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-11 & 7 \\\\ 8 & -5\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186419671} {:answer/id #uuid "d0704113-7386-54ca-ad0c-7578acd9ac62" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-3 & -11 \\\\ 2 & 7\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186419676} {:answer/id #uuid "dc81c6b4-cc09-56a5-916c-e323cc4197ee" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}9 & -1 \\\\ -3 & 9\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186419678}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186419667} :answer-field/id #uuid "32c807d8-09ff-597f-a633-55d26150ec43" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186419681}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "662e0d41-a6b8-58b3-ad4a-e835c48f17e2" :question/math-academy-id "q-97118" :question/problem "$$\nB=\\{[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]\\},\\,[T]_{B}=[\\begin{aligned}3 & -1 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the basis $B$ of ${R}^{2}$ and the matrix $[T]_{B}$ of a linear transformation $T$ with respect to this basis. Find the matrix of $T$ relative to the standard basis $S.$" :question/worked-solution "The connection between the matrices of our linear transformation $T$ relative to the basis $B$ and standard basis $S$ is given by the following diagram:\n\n![](images/db/dbd020362f950fe8bfb4c592652b9e78a73610fa01a247b0b7ead6f201bf9ea5.png)\n\n \n\nHere, moving along an arrow means pre-multiplying by the corresponding matrix. Algebraically, we can write this as\n\n$$\n[T]_{S}={P}_{B\\to S}\\cdot [T]_{B}\\cdot {P}_{B\\to S}^{-1},\n$$\n\nand the change-of-coordinates matrix from $B$ to $S$ is\n\n$$\n{P}_{B\\to S}=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 3\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFirst, we compute the inverse of ${P}_{B\\to S}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}_{B\\to S}^{-1} & ={[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 3\\end{aligned}]}^{-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3\\cdot 1-2\\cdot 2}[\\begin{aligned}3 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 2 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can compute the matrix of $T$ relative to the standard basis $S:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[T]_{S} & ={P}_{B\\to S}\\cdot [T]_{B}\\cdot {P}_{B\\to S}^{-1} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 3\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}3 & -1 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-3 & 2 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-3 & 2 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}5 & -3 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "0b2e1e0a-a93e-5b2b-ba4a-b6011da00e9b" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186419689}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186419688 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129640 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "03a7c2b7-b4a3-5168-84dc-aab27f106193" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448614} {:answer/id #uuid "2902f1ff-cac7-5d97-a5a9-24543413f25f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448615}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186448615} :answer-field/id #uuid "918a8f88-e411-59fb-b196-0dadf7f4fcbc" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449347} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "533a5b8f-639e-5a45-8484-cb33ae7d5e46" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448616} {:answer/id #uuid "e69f24f0-07c6-5983-ac28-bd000fd09e27" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448617}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186448616} :answer-field/id #uuid "54d6a6ae-1fa2-556d-9175-9c73166225ee" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449348} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c2c7db1c-97d8-53db-84ae-5422607ceccf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448618} {:answer/id #uuid "3c254231-3e39-568b-8359-80c766da4887" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448619}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186448619} :answer-field/id #uuid "db2048e9-209c-59b8-a528-935ef72da977" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449349}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "4a7452af-fe2e-5944-8a03-a396c12645a4" :question/math-academy-id "q-38360" :question/problem "Let $[T]_{B}$ and $[T]_{C}$ be the matrices of a linear transformation $T$ relative to the bases $B$ and $C,$ respectively. If ${P}_{B\\to C}$ is the change-of-coordinates matrix from basis $B$ to basis $C,$ which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $[T]_{C}={P}_{B\\to C}\\cdot [T]_{B}\\cdot {P}_{B\\to C}^{-1}$\n- **II.** $[T]_{C}={P}_{B\\to C}^{-1}\\cdot [T]_{B}\\cdot {P}_{B\\to C}$\n- **III.** $[T]_{B}={P}_{B\\to C}^{-1}\\cdot [T]_{C}\\cdot {P}_{B\\to C}$" :question/worked-solution "The connection between the matrices of our linear transformation $T$ relative to the bases $B$ and $C$ is given by the following diagram:\n\n![](images/1b/1b81504d6fdcabee0e1f586fef3dd2af5a57a8411ba4d22429e00e09b171a4d1.png)\n\nHere, moving along an arrow means pre-multiplying by the corresponding matrix. Algebraically, we can write this as\n\n$$\n[T]_{C}={P}_{B\\to C}\\cdot [T]_{B}\\cdot {P}_{B\\to C}^{-1},\n$$\n\nwhere ${P}_{B\\to C}$ is the change-of-coordinates matrix from $B$ to $C.$\n\nSolving this equation for $[T]_{B},$ we obtain the alternative formulation\n\n$$\n[T]_{B}={P}_{B\\to C}^{-1}\\cdot [T]_{C}\\cdot {P}_{B\\to C}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\""} :step/id #uuid "1b0f643e-dd6c-5eaf-875c-1b54d4c6e171" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186419690}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186419689 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186419685 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "094350f2-e017-53e0-b0e9-5075268dbe4d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}7 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 2\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186419659} {:answer/id #uuid "248c47da-3dc5-5333-809d-acf62be00f30" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-5 & -12 \\\\ 7 & 14\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186419663} {:answer/id #uuid "7d527527-44f7-5758-b7fd-4c2cd30a0912" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 9 \\\\ 0 & -7\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186419670} {:answer/id #uuid "c7cdd80b-8123-585f-9ada-143e7f38d137" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-5 & 6 \\\\ 1 & -4\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186419674} {:answer/id #uuid "cd75b99f-ca84-5149-9b32-732c0d712c4b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-11 & -18 \\\\ -9 & -16\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186419675}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186419663} :answer-field/id #uuid "2c3a3177-a079-5cdb-a624-7225c5f182a3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186419680}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "7afd5a54-c223-5e70-9582-9406545b2d06" :question/math-academy-id "q-97279" :question/problem "$$\nB=\\{[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}]\\},\\,C=\\{[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]\\},\\,[T]_{B}=[\\begin{aligned}4 & 3 \\\\ 2 & 5\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the bases $B$ and $C$ of ${R}^{2},$ and the matrix $[T]_{B}$ of a linear transformation $T$ with respect to the basis $B$, shown above. Find the matrix of $T$ relative to the basis $C.$" :question/worked-solution "The connection between the matrices of our linear transformation $T$ relative to the bases $B$ and $C$ is given by the following diagram:\n\n![](images/1b/1b81504d6fdcabee0e1f586fef3dd2af5a57a8411ba4d22429e00e09b171a4d1.png)\n\n \n\nHere, moving along an arrow means pre-multiplying by the corresponding matrix. Algebraically, we can write this as\n\n$$\n[T]_{C}={P}_{B\\to C}\\cdot [T]_{B}\\cdot {P}_{B\\to C}^{-1},\n$$\n\nwhere ${P}_{B\\to C}$ is the change-of-coordinates matrix from $B$ to $C.$\n\nFirst, let's denote the vectors of $B$ and $C$ as $\\{{b}_{1},{b}_{2}\\}$ and $\\{{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\}$, respectively.\n\nThe change-of-coordinates matrix from $B$ to $C$ is a matrix whose columns are the coordinates of vectors from $B$ relative to the basis $C.$\n\nHence, we need to find the coordinates of ${b}_{1}$ and ${b}_{2}$ relative to the basis $C.$ To do this, we consider the matrix\n\n$$\nM=[\\begin{aligned}| & | & | & | \\\\ {c}_{1} & {c}_{2} & {b}_{1} & {b}_{2} \\\\ | & | & | & |\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nWe reduce $M$ using Gaussian elimination until we get the identity matrix on the left. By doing this, we obtain the required change-of-coordinates matrix on the right.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 0 & -1 \\\\ -2 & -1 & 3 & -4\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+2{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 0 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 3 & -6\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :=\\frac{1}{3}{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 0 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 & -2\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & :={R}_{1}+(-2){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & -2 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 & -2\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence,\n\n$$\n{P}_{B\\to C}=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 3 \\\\ 1 & -2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the inverse of ${P}_{B\\to C}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}_{B\\to C}^{-1} & ={[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 3 \\\\ 1 & -2\\end{aligned}]}^{-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{(-2)\\cdot (-2)-1\\cdot 3}[\\begin{aligned}-2 & -3 \\\\ -1 & -2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 & -3 \\\\ -1 & -2\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can compute the matrix of $T$ relative to the basis $C:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[T]_{C} & ={P}_{B\\to C}\\cdot [T]_{B}\\cdot {P}_{B\\to C}^{-1} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 3 \\\\ 1 & -2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}4 & 3 \\\\ 2 & 5\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-2 & -3 \\\\ -1 & -2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 9 \\\\ 0 & -7\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-2 & -3 \\\\ -1 & -2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-5 & -12 \\\\ 7 & 14\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "49084fac-fe76-5f2f-8b13-058b89e81921" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186419691}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186419690 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186419686 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "573ba886-20ef-53c9-9024-a795a327b2ea" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454244} {:answer/id #uuid "75fa156d-1a39-536a-ab92-cd46c3d568a6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454245}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186454245} :answer-field/id #uuid "6c352d4a-a883-55eb-8913-4568682a7132" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454872} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a493b3a0-15e1-540d-9b9a-49e179799c67" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454246} {:answer/id #uuid "6b0b13f5-b674-5518-b581-93c06913cf58" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454247}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186454246} :answer-field/id #uuid "139440a5-d4e1-5ab8-ba4c-c3d86f2c0c74" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454873} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "41af9a8e-a9d1-5b00-b3e8-b95a4c022fcf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454248} {:answer/id #uuid "3a437d50-f3c9-5ac5-8e9a-de9b7e0b61f0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454249}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186454249} :answer-field/id #uuid "08c42cdc-f60a-5dff-99c5-7c1367615b5d" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454874}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "e737a421-d643-51f0-9d67-b9556fcc15c6" :question/math-academy-id "q-97060" :question/problem "Let $[T]_{B}$ and $[T]_{C}$ be the matrices of a linear transformation $T$ relative to the bases $B$ and $C,$ respectively. If ${P}_{B\\to C}$ is the change-of-coordinates matrix from basis $B$ to basis $C,$ which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $[T]_{B}={P}_{B\\to C}^{-1}\\cdot [T]_{C}\\cdot {P}_{B\\to C}$\n- **II.** $[T]_{C}={P}_{B\\to C}^{-1}\\cdot [T]_{B}\\cdot {P}_{B\\to C}$\n- **III.** $[T]_{C}={P}_{B\\to C}\\cdot [T]_{B}\\cdot {P}_{B\\to C}^{-1}$" :question/worked-solution "The connection between the matrices of our linear transformation $T$ relative to the bases $B$ and $C$ is given by the following diagram:\n\n![](images/1b/1b81504d6fdcabee0e1f586fef3dd2af5a57a8411ba4d22429e00e09b171a4d1.png)\n\n \n\nHere, moving along an arrow means pre-multiplying by the corresponding matrix. Algebraically, we can write this as\n\n$$\n[T]_{C}={P}_{B\\to C}\\cdot [T]_{B}\\cdot {P}_{B\\to C}^{-1},\n$$\n\nwhere ${P}_{B\\to C}$ is the change-of-coordinates matrix from $B$ to $C.$\n\nSolving this equation for $[T]_{B},$ we obtain the alternative formulation\n\n$$\n[T]_{B}={P}_{B\\to C}^{-1}\\cdot [T]_{C}\\cdot {P}_{B\\to C}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\""} :step/id #uuid "9a18e940-4aa2-5db0-8cbe-98d6ec06c6cd" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186419687}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186419691 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186419683 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3cd07fe6-6fc8-5bf5-a02a-2d9be90b12af" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}7 & 6 \\\\ -14 & -10\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186419666} {:answer/id #uuid "5e5ddfaa-301e-59f4-a460-764955ae24fe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}10 & 6 \\\\ -14 & -7\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186419668} {:answer/id #uuid "6431b86f-886e-5d1c-a26b-505391b5eaa1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}10 & -6 \\\\ 14 & 9\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186419669} {:answer/id #uuid "9029cfa0-da2b-5680-a6e9-bd10f503ed2f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}6 & 10 \\\\ -14 & -7\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186419672} {:answer/id #uuid "b7bd80ae-8e71-5474-9567-c47f73ef5af4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}10 & -7 \\\\ -14 & 6\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186419673}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186419668} :answer-field/id #uuid "80639a3b-2c96-57ee-bd01-981e0851478b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186419682}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "426fab7e-1490-581a-8c87-fe143e595ca4" :question/math-academy-id "q-140453" :question/problem "$$\n[T]_{B}=[\\begin{aligned}2 & -3 \\\\ 4 & 1\\end{aligned}],\\,{P}_{B\\to C}=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the matrix $[T]_{B}$ of a linear transformation $T$ with respect to the basis $B$ and the change-of-coordinates matrix ${P}_{B\\to C}$ shown above. Find the matrix of $T$ relative to the basis $C.$" :question/worked-solution "The connection between the matrices of our linear transformation $T$ relative to the bases $B$ and $C$ is given by the following diagram:\n\n![](images/1b/1b81504d6fdcabee0e1f586fef3dd2af5a57a8411ba4d22429e00e09b171a4d1.png)\n\n \n\nHere, moving along an arrow means pre-multiplying by the corresponding matrix. Algebraically, we can write this as\n\n$$\n[T]_{C}={P}_{B\\to C}\\cdot [T]_{B}\\cdot {P}_{B\\to C}^{-1}\\,,\n$$\n\nwhere ${P}_{B\\to C}$ is the change-of-coordinates matrix from $B$ to $C.$\n\nOur goal is to compute $[T]_{C}.$ First, we compute the inverse of ${P}_{B\\to C}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}_{B\\to C}^{-1} & ={[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{aligned}]}^{-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{1\\cdot 1-(-2)\\cdot (-1)}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & -1 \\\\ -2 & -1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can compute the matrix of $T$ relative to the basis $C:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[T]_{C} & ={P}_{B\\to C}\\cdot [T]_{B}\\cdot {P}_{B\\to C}^{-1} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 & -3 \\\\ 4 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-1 & -1 \\\\ -2 & -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 & -4 \\\\ 0 & 7\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-1 & -1 \\\\ -2 & -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}10 & 6 \\\\ -14 & -7\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "bf368b08-5b21-5ea2-9201-b8d34ec52b51"}] :activity/title "Connections Between Matrix Representations of a Linear Transformation" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186419658}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046659} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186488305} :activity/id #uuid "66f1714e-7bb9-59f0-b954-2026d5c8080b" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050324 :topic/id #uuid "3ffd42a4-cf3d-5ec4-9be0-19d427299cac" :topic/math-academy-id 2014} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186488295 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272105 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "16bb7c90-dd67-560d-bc55-0e6d60ffa1a8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-8 & -1 \\\\ 9 & 5\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269459} {:answer/id #uuid "24f93a4c-04c3-5326-b385-3b237af5be9f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-8 & -39 \\\\ 1 & 5\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269595} {:answer/id #uuid "45ce526b-cdce-5887-87fa-46e772b51158" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-8 & 5 \\\\ 39 & 5\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269907} {:answer/id #uuid "4d2b8368-af12-5270-8abb-2966b96811f7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 39 \\\\ 8 & -5\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269962} {:answer/id #uuid "52a4f459-56be-5d2b-aa62-980c1e97fec0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-39 & 8 \\\\ 5 & 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270004}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186269595} :answer-field/id #uuid "0f4347d9-b9dd-5363-a53a-02a14e4ccbbc" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271532}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "ac66a375-3ba5-552a-83b4-35e3ded13468" :question/math-academy-id "q-140482" :question/problem "$$\nB=\\{[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]\\},\\,[T]_{B}=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 1 \\\\ 3 & -1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the basis $B$ of ${R}^{2}$ and the matrix $[T]_{B}$ of a linear transformation $T$ with respect to this basis. Find the matrix of $T$ relative to the standard basis $S.$" :question/worked-solution "The connection between the matrices of our linear transformation $T$ relative to the basis $B$ and standard basis $S$ is given by the following diagram:\n\n![](images/db/dbd020362f950fe8bfb4c592652b9e78a73610fa01a247b0b7ead6f201bf9ea5.png)\n\nHere, moving along an arrow means pre-multiplying by the corresponding matrix. Algebraically, we can write this as\n\n$$\n[T]_{S}={P}_{B\\to S}\\cdot [T]_{B}\\cdot {P}_{B\\to S}^{-1},\n$$\n\nand the change-of-coordinates matrix from $B$ to $S$ is\n\n$$\n{P}_{B\\to S}=[\\begin{aligned}4 & 5 \\\\ -1 & -1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFirst, we compute the inverse of ${P}_{B\\to S}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}_{B\\to S}^{-1} & ={[\\begin{aligned}4 & 5 \\\\ -1 & -1\\end{aligned}]}^{-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4\\cdot (-1)-(-1)\\cdot 5}[\\begin{aligned}-1 & -5 \\\\ 1 & 4\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & -5 \\\\ 1 & 4\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can compute the matrix of $T$ relative to the standard basis $S:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[T]_{S} & ={P}_{B\\to S}\\cdot [T]_{B}\\cdot {P}_{B\\to S}^{-1} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}4 & 5 \\\\ -1 & -1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-2 & 1 \\\\ 3 & -1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-1 & -5 \\\\ 1 & 4\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}7 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-1 & -5 \\\\ 1 & 4\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-8 & -39 \\\\ 1 & 5\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "2d7e0158-2616-5cb3-ae9b-81b04c5bccd8"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186488305 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129697 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "34b8c4b6-b53f-5fd6-8a59-c4c18583dd9e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-25 & -14 \\\\ 16 & 9\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392233} {:answer/id #uuid "eaa442b6-3b37-5157-88fd-f23ac8a4d42a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}5 & -18 \\\\ 2 & -7\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392234} {:answer/id #uuid "700fe7c1-5ebe-5a72-b683-b35432a45b97" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-3 & -2 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392235} {:answer/id #uuid "7362374a-a152-5b2b-adfe-f73b7788011b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}3 & -10 \\\\ -4 & 13\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392236} {:answer/id #uuid "991e6247-97fb-5467-ad97-075e201709bd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}11 & 6 \\\\ -20 & -11\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392237}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186392234} :answer-field/id #uuid "1ffe2e2a-b586-5e41-933b-844875782379" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392232}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "ef695398-bdfe-5a30-85b6-97e0a8733294" :question/math-academy-id "q-38761" :question/problem "$$\nB=\\{[\\begin{aligned}11 \\\\ -5\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}]\\},\\,C=\\{[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\},\\,[T]_{B}=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ -2 & -3\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the bases $B$ and $C$ of ${R}^{2},$ and the matrix $[T]_{B}$ of a linear transformation $T$ with respect to the basis $B$, shown above. Find the matrix of $T$ relative to the basis $C.$" :question/worked-solution "The connection between the matrices of our linear transformation $T$ relative to the bases $B$ and $C$ is given by the following diagram:\n\n![](images/1b/1b81504d6fdcabee0e1f586fef3dd2af5a57a8411ba4d22429e00e09b171a4d1.png)\n\n \n\nHere, moving along an arrow means pre-multiplying by the corresponding matrix. Algebraically, we can write this as\n\n$$\n[T]_{C}={P}_{B\\to C}\\cdot [T]_{B}\\cdot {P}_{B\\to C}^{-1},\n$$\n\nwhere ${P}_{B\\to C}$ is the change-of-coordinates matrix from $B$ to $C.$\n\nFirst, let's denote the vectors of $B$ and $C$ as $\\{{b}_{1},{b}_{2}\\}$ and $\\{{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\}$, respectively.\n\nThe change-of-coordinates matrix from $B$ to $C$ is a matrix whose columns are the coordinates of vectors from $B$ relative to the basis $C.$\n\nHence, we need to find the coordinates of ${b}_{1}$ and ${b}_{2}$ relative to the basis $C.$ To do this, we consider the matrix\n\n$$\nM=[\\begin{aligned}| & | & | & | \\\\ {c}_{1} & {c}_{2} & {b}_{1} & {b}_{2} \\\\ | & | & | & |\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nWe reduce $M$ using Gaussian elimination until we get the identity matrix on the left. By doing this, we obtain the required change-of-coordinates matrix on the right.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 11 & 6 \\\\ -1 & 1 & -5 & -3\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 11 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 6 & 3\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :=\\frac{1}{3}{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 11 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & :={R}_{1}+(-2){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 7 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence,\n\n$$\n{P}_{B\\to C}=[\\begin{aligned}7 & 4 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the inverse of ${P}_{B\\to C}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}_{B\\to C}^{-1} & ={[\\begin{aligned}7 & 4 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}]}^{-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{1\\cdot 7-2\\cdot 4}[\\begin{aligned}1 & -4 \\\\ -2 & 7\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 4 \\\\ 2 & -7\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can compute the matrix of $T$ relative to the basis $C:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[T]_{C} & ={P}_{B\\to C}\\cdot [T]_{B}\\cdot {P}_{B\\to C}^{-1} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}7 & 4 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ -2 & -3\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-1 & 4 \\\\ 2 & -7\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-1 & 4 \\\\ 2 & -7\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}5 & -18 \\\\ 2 & -7\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "511ccafa-e28b-5ce5-8ce9-e4025e74a362" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186488307}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186488307 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186395229 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0609b365-00a9-5e3f-b18a-4ae8f0cb6423" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 7 \\\\ -6 & 9\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395231} {:answer/id #uuid "900a056b-ff09-5fb7-93ec-d70236eeac1b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}5 & 7 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395232} {:answer/id #uuid "5e60741c-2d26-5cbb-b653-d42461c6724c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-7 & 3 \\\\ -3 & 2\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395233} {:answer/id #uuid "a567a6a7-4ff1-59da-a544-576e1192accc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}5 & 9 \\\\ 5 & 6\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395234} {:answer/id #uuid "c2fc3385-8e0c-58d4-8ac0-fc0beafee826" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}9 & -1 \\\\ -8 & 7\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395235}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186395232} :answer-field/id #uuid "161f0cc1-c732-5e8a-bdbf-03f067c9d5be" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395230}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "65dae103-c225-5d3f-8973-42e2dd0c3a80" :question/math-academy-id "q-97115" :question/problem "$$\n[T]_{C}=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 3 & 4\\end{aligned}],\\,{P}_{C\\to B}=[\\begin{aligned}-5 & 2 \\\\ 3 & -1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the matrix $[T]_{C}$ of a linear transformation $T$ with respect to the basis $C$ and the change-of-coordinates matrix ${P}_{C\\to B}$ shown above. Find the matrix of $T$ relative to the basis $B.$" :question/worked-solution "The connection between the matrices of our linear transformation $T$ relative to the bases $B$ and $C$ is given by the following diagram:\n\n![](images/2e/2e82c232614bec8a98f079ea56d7913ee13e7b457e33e418198278d2e369fc5c.png)\n\nHere, moving along an arrow means pre-multiplying by the corresponding matrix. Algebraically, we can write this as\n\n$$\n[T]_{C}={P}_{C\\to B}^{-1}\\cdot [T]_{B}\\cdot {P}_{C\\to B}.\n$$\n\nwhere ${P}_{C\\to B}$ is the change-of-coordinates matrix from $C$ to $B.$\n\nOur goal is to compute $[T]_{B}.$ Solving this equation for $[T]_{B},$ we obtain the alternative formulation\n\n$$\n[T]_{B}={P}_{C\\to B}\\cdot [T]_{C}\\cdot {P}_{C\\to B}^{-1}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we compute the inverse of ${P}_{C\\to B}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}_{C\\to B}^{-1} & ={[\\begin{aligned}-5 & 2 \\\\ 3 & -1\\end{aligned}]}^{-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{(-1)\\cdot (-5)-3\\cdot 2}[\\begin{aligned}-1 & -2 \\\\ -3 & -5\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 5\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can compute the matrix of $T$ relative to the basis $B:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[T]_{B} & ={P}_{C\\to B}\\cdot [T]_{C}\\cdot {P}_{C\\to B}^{-1} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-5 & 2 \\\\ 3 & -1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 3 & 4\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 5\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-4 & 3 \\\\ 3 & -1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 5\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}5 & 7 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "5cef157a-71ae-54ad-924f-79f27027d30c" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186488330}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186488330 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360754 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "182077b4-5f2c-531e-96ce-4b88e8ab4d4c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-4 & 10 \\\\ -5 & 13\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357964} {:answer/id #uuid "42fae3bb-9ef9-579c-b81a-3be55f9c0d12" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-6 & 16 \\\\ -11 & 2\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358324} {:answer/id #uuid "6ec1f99a-0977-5b71-97c0-662f0436dfd1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 8 \\\\ -9 & 5\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358716} {:answer/id #uuid "be23d718-4726-5599-970d-363a78d2bd57" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}2 & -4 \\\\ -1 & 3\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359411} {:answer/id #uuid "de1c3f3a-b06a-5be2-a7ff-4c20229cd2ae" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}6 & -4 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359703}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186359411} :answer-field/id #uuid "215bd893-15dc-5185-8467-98be175ac074" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360051}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "143b7701-47b4-58ee-98d5-de35b377edb1" :question/math-academy-id "q-97119" :question/problem "$$\nB=\\{[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\},\\,[T]_{B}=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 3\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the basis $B$ of ${R}^{2}$ and the matrix $[T]_{B}$ of a linear transformation $T$ with respect to this basis. Find the matrix of $T$ relative to the standard basis $S.$" :question/worked-solution "The connection between the matrices of our linear transformation $T$ relative to the basis $B$ and standard basis $S$ is given by the following diagram:\n\n![](images/db/dbd020362f950fe8bfb4c592652b9e78a73610fa01a247b0b7ead6f201bf9ea5.png)\n\n \n\nHere, moving along an arrow means pre-multiplying by the corresponding matrix. Algebraically, we can write this as\n\n$$\n[T]_{S}={P}_{B\\to S}\\cdot [T]_{B}\\cdot {P}_{B\\to S}^{-1},\n$$\n\nand the change-of-coordinates matrix from $B$ to $S$ is\n\n$$\n{P}_{B\\to S}=[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 2 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFirst, we compute the inverse of ${P}_{B\\to S}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}_{B\\to S}^{-1} & ={[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 2 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}]}^{-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{1\\cdot (-3)-(-1)\\cdot 2}[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 \\\\ 1 & -3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 2 \\\\ -1 & 3\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can compute the matrix of $T$ relative to the standard basis $S:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[T]_{S} & ={P}_{B\\to S}\\cdot [T]_{B}\\cdot {P}_{B\\to S}^{-1} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 2 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 3\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-1 & 2 \\\\ -1 & 3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-1 & 2 \\\\ -1 & 3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & -4 \\\\ -1 & 3\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "be618910-dbda-5153-91ac-000b728f4deb" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186488334}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186488334 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186395263 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6f012443-8bd4-52ba-9493-3919a35ddba8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395265} {:answer/id #uuid "47fce7e4-6451-5159-ab17-02eca79cf3cd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-5 & 6 \\\\ -6 & 7\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395266} {:answer/id #uuid "77558324-5aee-52a7-9c40-5a51f9aaca4f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}7 & -6 \\\\ 6 & -5\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395267} {:answer/id #uuid "9411351f-be5d-5884-a210-6d5c30bf0df5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 3 \\\\ -3 & 4\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395268} {:answer/id #uuid "3e925a83-495e-56e8-8d9b-c8d313746fdb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}4 & -3 \\\\ 3 & -2\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395269}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186395268} :answer-field/id #uuid "b2c44b4f-f29e-547e-a303-1a31ffccb3ca" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395264}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "90dac6d1-6fdb-5236-89cf-8823a20cc32a" :question/math-academy-id "q-97281" :question/problem "$$\nB=\\{[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\\},\\,C=\\{[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}]\\},\\,[T]_{B}=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 3 \\\\ -3 & 4\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the bases $B$ and $C$ of ${R}^{2},$ and the matrix $[T]_{B}$ of a linear transformation $T$ with respect to the basis $B$, shown above. Find the matrix of $T$ relative to the basis $C.$" :question/worked-solution "The connection between the matrices of our linear transformation $T$ relative to the bases $B$ and $C$ is given by the following diagram:\n\n![](images/1b/1b81504d6fdcabee0e1f586fef3dd2af5a57a8411ba4d22429e00e09b171a4d1.png)\n\n \n\nHere, moving along an arrow means pre-multiplying by the corresponding matrix. Algebraically, we can write this as\n\n$$\n[T]_{C}={P}_{B\\to C}\\cdot [T]_{B}\\cdot {P}_{B\\to C}^{-1},\n$$\n\nwhere ${P}_{B\\to C}$ is the change-of-coordinates matrix from $B$ to $C.$\n\nFirst, let's denote the vectors of $B$ and $C$ as $\\{{b}_{1},{b}_{2}\\}$ and $\\{{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\}$, respectively.\n\nThe change-of-coordinates matrix from $B$ to $C$ is a matrix whose columns are the coordinates of vectors from $B$ relative to the basis $C.$\n\nHence, we need to find the coordinates of ${b}_{1}$ and ${b}_{2}$ relative to the basis $C.$ To do this, we consider the matrix\n\n$$\nM=[\\begin{aligned}| & | & | & | \\\\ {c}_{1} & {c}_{2} & {b}_{1} & {b}_{2} \\\\ | & | & | & |\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nWe reduce $M$ using Gaussian elimination until we get the identity matrix on the left. By doing this, we obtain the required change-of-coordinates matrix on the right.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 & -2 & 1 \\\\ 2 & -3 & -1 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-2){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 & -2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 3 & -2\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & :={R}_{1}+2{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 4 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 3 & -2\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence,\n\n$$\n{P}_{B\\to C}=[\\begin{aligned}4 & -3 \\\\ 3 & -2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the inverse of ${P}_{B\\to C}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}_{B\\to C}^{-1} & ={[\\begin{aligned}4 & -3 \\\\ 3 & -2\\end{aligned}]}^{-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4\\cdot (-2)-(-3)\\cdot 3}[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 3 \\\\ -3 & 4\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 3 \\\\ -3 & 4\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can compute the matrix of $T$ relative to the basis $C:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[T]_{C} & ={P}_{B\\to C}\\cdot [T]_{B}\\cdot {P}_{B\\to C}^{-1} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}4 & -3 \\\\ 3 & -2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-2 & 3 \\\\ -3 & 4\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-2 & 3 \\\\ -3 & 4\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-2 & 3 \\\\ -3 & 4\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 3 \\\\ -3 & 4\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "cb8b79c9-9224-5432-a419-5e30ce55873d" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186488295}}] :activity/title "Connections Between Matrix Representations of a Linear Transformation" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186487557}]}
